[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that were specifically probed as likely divergence points
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places a translation usually drifts. Each was tested and each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already matched — they are recorded so a later reader can re-check them rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than rediscover them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`fgets` truncation at 255 bytes, remainder retained.** `char input[256]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    means an over-long line is *split*, not discarded. The sharpest case:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    254 spaces + `"77\n"` — `fgets` returns 254 spaces plus the *first* `7`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the choice is 7 and the program exits without ever seeing the second `7`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    At 253 spaces the same bytes parse as `77` → `"Invalid choice"`. Also
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    covered: a 255-byte payload leaving the `\n` behind as an extra empty line,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and 300 zeros followed by `7` (first line → choice 0, remainder → choice 7).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `stdio::fgets` reproduces all of this.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **`sscanf("%d")` overflow.** glibc converts via `strtol` and, on overflow,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    saturates to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` (setting `ERANGE`) before the result is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    truncated to `int`. It does **not** wrap modulo 2^64. Discriminating cases:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `18446744073709551617` (2^64+1) and `18446744073709551621` (2^64+5) —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    wrapping would select demo 1 and demo 5, saturation yields `(int)-1` →
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `"Invalid choice"`. Both programs print `"Invalid choice"`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `stdio::sscanf_int` matches by saturating to `i64::MIN/MAX` and then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    casting `as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **`int` truncation of in-range `long` values.** `4294967303` (2^32+7)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    truncates to 7 and *exits*; `8589934598` (2·2^32+6) truncates to 6 and runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    every demo; `2147483648` truncates to `INT_MIN`. All match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **`sscanf` lexical details.** Leading whitespace is skipped (including
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `\v` and `\f`), an optional `+`/`-` is accepted, leading zeros are ignored,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and conversion stops at the first non-digit (`3abc` → 3, `6.9` → 6,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `0x10` → 0, `1 2` → 1). All match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **Embedded NUL bytes.** `fgets` copies a NUL through as an ordinary byte,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    after which `sscanf` sees a C string that ends there. `"\0" "7\n"` is an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    input failure; `"7\0\n"` still converts to 7. Both match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **Invalid UTF-8 on stdin and non-ASCII in output.** Input is treated as raw
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    bytes on both sides (`0xff 0xfe`, lone high bytes, full-width `７`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Output-side: the `°` in demo 2 and the box-drawing banner in `main` are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    multi-byte UTF-8 in the C source and are emitted as the same bytes by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Rust build. Item names are printed through `out_raw` on the NUL-terminated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    byte buffer, so no transcoding can occur.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **`printf("%.2f")` vs Rust `{:.2}` rounding.** Verified independently
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    against tie-prone values (`0.125`, `2.675`, `1.005`, `0.5`, `1.5`, `2.5`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `8.995`, …) at both `%.2f` and `%.1f`: glibc and Rust produce identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    digits, because both round the exact binary value half-to-even. No
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    divergence risk here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **The `max_price` quirk in `calculate_inventory_stats`.** C seeds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `max_price = 0.0` while seeding `min_price` from `items->data[0].price`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    This is asymmetric and would report `0.00` as the maximum for an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    all-negative-price inventory. The Rust version reproduces it verbatim
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (`inventory.rs`) rather than "fixing" it to seed from element 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Paths that no input can reach through the executable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Several `inventory.c` branches are dead with respect to `main()`, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m black-box differential run cannot cover them. They were checked by reading the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m two sources side by side instead; each is a faithful translation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `calculate_inventory_stats` `size == 0` → `"No items in inventory"`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `calculate_order_stats` `size == 0` → `"No orders to analyze"`. `main()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   always populates 10 items / 8 orders first.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `find_items_by_category` `found == 0` → `"No items found in this category"`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `main()` only ever queries `"Electronics"` and `"Furniture"`, which both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `find_expensive_items` is never called from `main()` at all — neither
